/// The `wa-whatsapp` component's own world: the kernel service contract plus the one queue seam the
/// explicit-send outbox needs.
///
/// `include` pulls in `wa:kernel/service-component@0.2.0` unchanged (the guest imports
/// websocket/http/speech/kv/lifecycle/log/credentials and exports `service.run`), and the import below
/// is the outbox: `wa:queue/worker` only, because this component *consumes* `whatsapp.outbox` and never
/// produces on it (the node enqueues, `rust/wa-host/src/whatsapp_send.rs`). The manifest grants exactly
/// that interface on exactly that namespace (`manifest.json`, `docs/SERVICE-COMPONENTS.md`), so
/// importing more than the grant allows would be a refusal at the call, not a capability.
///
/// `deps/kernel.wit`, `deps/clocks.wit` and `deps/queue/queue.wit` are snapshots of `wit/kernel-v2/`
/// and `wit/queue/` (the same arrangement `rust/components/svc-queue-echo` uses): wit-bindgen resolves
/// a world's dependencies from `wit/deps/`, so a component that composes two contracts carries them.
/// A change to either published contract has to be re-copied here - `scripts/test-whatsapp-send.lua`
/// compares the copies byte for byte and fails when they drift.
package wa:whatsapp@0.1.0;

world whatsapp-service {
    include wa:kernel/service-component@0.2.0;
    import wa:queue/worker@0.1.0;
    // The OCR reader (`wit/deps/kernel.wit`, `interface vision`): the image text the index needs to
    // make a photo searchable by what it shows (`docs/WHATSAPP-OCR.md`). It is not one of the
    // service world's imports - that world is the obligations every 0.2 service has - so a component
    // that reads images opts in here, and the manifest's `ocr: true` is the grant behind the call.
    import wa:kernel/vision@0.2.0;
    // The drain (`wit/deps/kernel.wit`, `interface drain`): a hot swap asks this instance to finish
    // the send or index operation in hand and report idle before it stops it, so an upgrade does not
    // retry a job whose effect was already half-applied (`docs/SERVICE-COMPONENTS.md`, "A swap waits
    // for the work in flight"). Importing it is the declaration: the kernel reads the artifact's
    // imports, so a build without this line is stopped immediately, as before.
    import wa:kernel/drain@0.2.0;
}
